13:07:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've caught a real gap in my last answer—I overcorrected. You're right that material properties matter fundamentally, not just design choices. Let me be more precise: **The actual material science:** Glass and plastic fail in fundamentally different ways due to their molecular structure: - **Glass** is a rigid, amorphous solid. Under stress, it can't deform—it has essentially zero "give." When force exceeds its limit, it fails catastrophically and immediately (brittle fracture). All that stored energy releases at once, causing cracks to propagate rapidly. - **Plastic** (especially polycarbonate, which most rugged phones/kids' tablets use) has long polymer chains that can flex, stretch, and absorb impact energy through molecular deformation. It can bend before breaking, and even when it does fail, it tends to deform or crack partially rather than shattering. This is why rugged phones (like Cat phones, Kyocera) and kids' tablets (LeapFrog, etc.) use polycarbonate—it's genuinely, fundamentally more impact-resistant, regardless of design. You could put the exact same thin edge-to-edge screen design on a polycarbonate phone and it would survive drops that shatter glass. **So why do mainstream phones still use glass?** Given that plastic is more impact-resistant, why doesn't everyone use it? Because manufacturers are optimizing for a different set of priorities: 1. Scratch resistance (glass wins decisively) 2. Optical clarity/no yellowing over time (glass wins) 3. Premium feel/aesthetics (glass wins) 4. Impact resistance (plastic wins) Mainstream phone makers are betting that consumers care more about a scratch-free, clear, premium-feeling screen for daily use than surviving occasional drops. Rugged phone/kids' tablet makers make the opposite bet—durability against drops matters more than a pristine, scratch-free surface. **Bottom line:** I was wrong to imply it's just design tradeoffs. The material itself matters a lot—glass is inherently more brittle, plastic is inherently more impact-resistant. But manufacturers still choose glass for mainstream phones because they're prioritizing different failure modes (daily scratching) over others (occasional dropping), essentially betting that most users will tolerate/pay for a screen protector or case rather than sacrifice the glass "feel" and clarity.
