ints like your friend's story), but they're statistically less common than daily wear - Manufacturers are optimizing for the *aggregate* most common failure mode, even if it's less dramatic when it happens So you're right that if "durability" = "won't crack," plastic wins outright. But if "durability" = "maintains full functionality and clarity over years of typical use," it's genuinely a toss-up depending on how you use your phone—and that's a legitimate reason both material choices persist in the market rather than one fully displacing the other.
